{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The unconditional distribution of $X$ can be derived from conditional distributions $P(X|Y)$ by integrating over $Y$: $P(X) = \\int P(X|Y) P(Y) \\, dY$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:09:08\u201300:10:00] Now let us integrate both parts with respect to y. What will we obtain in the left-hand part expression? So the integral of conditional density, P of y given x with respect to y. What will it result to? Well, with respect to y, this is just probability density function. And the integral of probability density function always results in one. And this means that we obtain one equals P of x given y times P of y."
    },
    {
      "claim": "This integral expression is equivalent to taking the expectation of $P(X|Y)$ with respect to $Y$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:10:46\u201300:11:17] So it is the same as to say that this is expectation with respect to y over all conditional distributions."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The unconditional distribution $P(X)$ is obtained by averaging all conditional distributions $P(X|Y)$ over $Y$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:10:46\u201300:11:17] Imagine that you know all conditional distributions on x given arbitrary y's. And now you would like to obtain unconditional distribution on x. So how you can do this? You can simply take this integral and this integral is actually an expectation with respect to y."
    },
    {
      "claim": "This process can be generalized to multiple random variables by integrating out the remaining variables",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:11:49\u201300:12:44] by induction it's straightforward to prove that this goes through for arbitrary number of random variables so you have say distribution over x and x1 over x1 xn and we would like to get distribution over x1 xk we can easily do it just by taking joint distribution and integrating out remaining variables"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginalization process is analogous to integrating out $Y$ from the joint distribution $P(X,Y)$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:10:46\u201300:11:17] And we can also, if we remember the product rule, we can also note that this is the same as to say that this is just a joint distribution where y was integrated out."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The integral of the conditional density $P(Y|X)$ with respect to $Y$ results in 1",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:09:08\u201300:10:00] So the integral of conditional density, P of y given x with respect to y. What will it result to? Well, with respect to y, this is just probability density function. And the integral of probability density function always results in one."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginalization formula can be written as $P(X) = \\int P(X|Y) P(Y) \\, dY$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:09:08\u201300:10:00] And this means that we obtain one equals P of x given y times P of y."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The phrase \"U of x U of y\" in the source appears to be a transcription error for $P(X|Y)$ or similar",
      "supported": false,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:10:02\u201300:10:46] Sorry, one equals the integral u of y given x, u of x given y, u of y, over p of x dy. This is marked as uncertain in the notes."
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The source uses non-standard notation (e.g., 'u' for probability) and has transcription errors that may affect interpretation."
  }
